 1 big thing: House races shift toward Dems 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data : The Cook Political Report with Amy Walter. Chart: Erin Davis/Axios The Cook Political Report today shifted 18 more U.S. House races toward Democrats despite Republicans' massive ad spending.

 Democrats are now expected to gain nine to 19 seats, the nonpartisan forecast says. 🏛️ Cook's Erin Covey writes: "That would give them 224 seats on the lower end and 234 on the upper end." (218 seats make a House majority.) 

 "At this point, Democrats only need to win 20% of the [ 21 ] toss-ups to get to a majority, assuming the seats that favor Democrats in our ratings all break their way." The shifts include: 

 Five seats — in Iowa, Pennsylvania and Washington — moving from toss-ups to leaning Democratic, including Rep. Mariannette Miller-Meeks' (R-Iowa) tough rematch against Democrat ⁠Christina Bohannan. Cook nudged four races — in Iowa, Michigan, North Carolina and Virginia — from leaning Republican to toss-ups. That's good news for Rep. Don Davis (D-N.C.), whose district was warped by mid-decade redistricting. Republicans' hold on other races in Kentucky, Maine, Pennsylvania, Arkansas and Indiana slipped, shifting them to Lean R or Likely R. Democrats' hold on Nevada, New York and Nebraska races tightened. Go deeper ... Explore Cook's analysis . 

 
 
